## 1. Increase the Local Variable Array

The original Compiler structure contains:

```c
Local locals[UINT8_COUNT];
```

To allow more local variables, I would define
a larger limit.

In compiler.c:

```c
#define MAX_LOCALS 1024
```

Then update the Compiler structure:

```c
typedef struct {
  Local locals[MAX_LOCALS];
  int localCount;
  int scopeDepth;
} Compiler;
```

The compiler can now track up to 1,024 local
variables instead of only 256.

## 2. Update the Local Variable Limit Check

The addLocal() function checks whether the
compiler has room for another local variable.

Originally, this check uses UINT8_COUNT.

I would update it to use MAX_LOCALS.

```c
static void addLocal(Token name) {
  if (current->localCount == MAX_LOCALS) {
    error("Too many local variables in function.");
    return;
  }

  Local* local =
      &current->locals[current->localCount++];

  local->name = name;
  local->depth = -1;
}
```

This prevents the compiler from writing past the
end of the larger array.

The depth is initially set to -1 because
Chapter 22 uses that value to mark a local
variable that has not finished initializing.

## 3. Use Two-Byte Local Variable Operands

Increasing the local array alone is not enough.

The bytecode still needs to represent stack slots
with indexes greater than 255.

The original implementation emits one-byte
operands for OP_GET_LOCAL and OP_SET_LOCAL.

I would update these instructions to use two
bytes for their slot indexes.

A two-byte unsigned operand can represent
values from 0 to 65,535, which is more than
enough for the new 1,024-local limit.

First, add a helper function in compiler.c:

```c
static void emitShort(uint16_t value) {
  emitByte((value >> 8) & 0xff);
  emitByte(value & 0xff);
}
```

This stores a 16-bit number as two bytes,
with the most significant byte first.

The OP_GET_LOCAL and OP_SET_LOCAL instructions
would retain their original opcode names,
but now each would have a two-byte operand.

## 4. Update Local Variable Code Generation

The namedVariable() function determines whether
a variable should be accessed as a local
or a global variable.

The global instructions can continue using
their original one-byte operands.

For local variables, I would emit the opcode
followed by the two-byte stack slot index.

The relevant part of namedVariable() becomes:

```c
static void namedVariable(Token name, bool canAssign) {
  uint8_t getOp, setOp;

  int arg = resolveLocal(current, &name);
  bool isLocal = arg != -1;

  if (isLocal) {
    getOp = OP_GET_LOCAL;
    setOp = OP_SET_LOCAL;
  } else {
    arg = identifierConstant(&name);
    getOp = OP_GET_GLOBAL;
    setOp = OP_SET_GLOBAL;
  }

  if (canAssign && match(TOKEN_EQUAL)) {
    expression();

    emitByte(setOp);

    if (isLocal) {
      emitShort((uint16_t)arg);
    } else {
      emitByte((uint8_t)arg);
    }
  } else {
    emitByte(getOp);

    if (isLocal) {
      emitShort((uint16_t)arg);
    } else {
      emitByte((uint8_t)arg);
    }
  }
}
```

This allows the compiler to generate bytecode
for local variables with slot indexes above 255.

Global variable instructions are unchanged
because this challenge only concerns locals.

## 5. Update the Virtual Machine

The VM must also be able to read two-byte
operands for local variable instructions.

Inside run() in vm.c, add the following macro
alongside the existing byte-reading macros:

```c
#define READ_SHORT() \
  (vm.ip += 2, \
   (uint16_t)((vm.ip[-2] << 8) | vm.ip[-1]))
```

Then update OP_GET_LOCAL:

```c
case OP_GET_LOCAL: {
  uint16_t slot = READ_SHORT();
  push(vm.stack[slot]);
  break;
}
```

Update OP_SET_LOCAL:

```c
case OP_SET_LOCAL: {
  uint16_t slot = READ_SHORT();
  vm.stack[slot] = peek(0);
  break;
}
```

Previously, these instructions read only
one byte using READ_BYTE().

Now they read a 16-bit slot index.

This allows the VM to access local variables
stored beyond the original 256-slot limit.

The READ_SHORT macro should also be undefined
at the end of run(), along with the other
instruction-reading macros.

## 6. Increase the VM Stack Capacity

The original clox VM also has a fixed-size
stack that can hold 256 values.

Even if the compiler supports more locals,
the VM would run out of stack space unless
its capacity is increased.

In vm.h, change the stack limit to:

```c
#define STACK_MAX 4096
```

The VM's existing stack declaration can
continue using:

```c
Value stack[STACK_MAX];
```

This provides space for the 1,024 local
variables as well as additional temporary
values used when evaluating expressions.

For a production implementation, I would
also add stack overflow checks or use
dynamically growing stack storage.

## 7. Update the Disassembler

Since OP_GET_LOCAL and OP_SET_LOCAL now
have two-byte operands, the disassembler
must read them correctly.

In debug.c, add a helper function:

```c
static int shortInstruction(const char* name,
                            Chunk* chunk, int offset) {
  uint16_t slot =
      (uint16_t)(chunk->code[offset + 1] << 8);

  slot |= chunk->code[offset + 2];

  printf("%-16s %4u\n", name, (unsigned)slot);

  return offset + 3;
}
```

Then update the corresponding cases in
disassembleInstruction():

```c
case OP_GET_LOCAL:
  return shortInstruction("OP_GET_LOCAL",
                          chunk, offset);

case OP_SET_LOCAL:
  return shortInstruction("OP_SET_LOCAL",
                          chunk, offset);
```

The disassembler now advances by three bytes
for each local variable instruction:
one opcode byte and two operand bytes.

## 8. Example Test

To verify that the new design can handle
more than 256 local variables, I would
test a block containing 300 declarations.

For example, the generated Lox program
would look like:

```lox
{
  var a0 = 0;
  var a1 = 1;
  var a2 = 2;

  // Continue declaring variables...

  var a298 = 298;
  var a299 = 299;

  print a299;
}
```

Expected output:

```text
299
```

To generate the full test automatically,
I could use the following Python script:

```python
with open("test_many_locals.lox", "w") as file:
    file.write("{\n")

    for i in range(300):
        file.write(f"  var a{i} = {i};\n")

    file.write("  print a299;\n")
    file.write("}\n")
```

This test is useful because it creates
more local variables than the original
one-byte limit allows.

The test should also confirm that the
last variable can be accessed correctly.

Additional tests should verify assignment
to a high-numbered local slot, nested
scopes, and ordinary variable shadowing.

These are proposed tests, not recorded
test executions.

## 9. Performance and Memory Considerations

Using two-byte operands allows the VM
to access a much larger number of local
variables.

However, it also increases the size
of bytecode instructions for local
variable access and assignment.

Originally, OP_GET_LOCAL and OP_SET_LOCAL
used two bytes total: one for the opcode
and one for the operand.

With this design, each uses three bytes.

This slightly increases memory consumption
and the amount of bytecode the VM reads.

Increasing the compiler's local variable
array and the VM stack also requires
additional memory.

However, local variable access remains
efficient because the VM still uses
direct indexing into the stack.
